Latency:

Latency = how long a request takes to get a response

SLI (Service Level Indicator):

SLI = the actual measurement we track
Example: request response time

SLO (Service Level Objective):

SLO = the performance target we promise internally

Example:

“95% of requests should respond in under 300 ms”

SLO Breach:

SLO breach = the system did not meet the promised target

So it means:

Requests were slower than what we agreed was acceptable.

Alert Triggered by Latency SLO Breach

Alert triggered = monitoring detected user-impacting slowness

So this sentence means:

Users were experiencing noticeable slowness beyond acceptable limits, even though the system didn’t look busy at the CPU level.

CPU Usage Appeared Normal

CPU normal = servers were not overloaded in terms of compute

So it implies:

The problem wasn’t CPU — it was something else.

Connection Pool Exhaustion

Connection pool exhaustion = the application ran out of available connections (DB, downstream service, etc.)

Meaning:

Requests were waiting or failing even though CPU was free.

HPA Scaling Only on CPU

HPA on CPU = Kubernetes was scaling based only on CPU usage

So the issue was:

Traffic increased, but CPU didn’t — so autoscaling didn’t react.

Business Impact

Business impact = real users were affected

Examples:

Slow pages

Failed requests (5xx errors)

Lost revenue during campaign
